# Every variable this app actually reads. Verified against the source, not
# assumed — three variables that used to live here (RESEND_API_KEY,
# REMINDERS_FROM_EMAIL, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY) have been removed because
# nothing reads them any more.
#
# Note that most integration credentials are NOT here: Autotask, NinjaOne,
# M365 partner, Huntress, FortiCloud, Hudu, Wizer, NordLayer, NordPass and
# the AI provider key all live in singleton tables in the database and are
# configured under Settings → Integrations. An empty database therefore has
# every one of those integrations switched off, which is what makes a fresh
# staging project safe to point at the real world.

# --- Supabase (Project Settings -> API) --------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server-only, never exposed to the browser, and bypasses RLS entirely.
# Used by createAdminClient() for cron routes and any action that has to
# read across users.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Public URL ---------------------------------------------------------
# Baked in AT BUILD TIME, not read at runtime: changing it needs a rebuild,
# not just a variable edit. Required because Railway's proxy makes
# request.url resolve to an internal localhost:8080 rather than the public
# domain. If unset, links inside outbound email come out as
# http://localhost:3000 (or empty) — see src/lib/app-url.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://your-app.up.railway.app

# --- Environment label --------------------------------------------------
# Optional. When set, every page renders a banner with this text. Leave
# unset in production; set to STAGING (or similar) anywhere else, so an
# environment that looks identical to production announces itself.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENV_LABEL=STAGING

# --- Cron ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret each Railway Cron Job sends as the X-Cron-Secret header.
# All 11 scheduled routes fail closed: if this is unset, every one of them
# returns 401 and nothing scheduled runs. That is the intended state for a
# staging environment — leave it blank there.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Microsoft 365 / Entra ----------------------------------------------
# One app registration, used for "Sign in with Microsoft", per-user mailbox
# connect, and app-only shared-mailbox send. Each deployment's own
# /auth/callback and /api/mail/callback URLs must be registered on it as
# redirect URIs.
AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=
AZURE_TENANT_ID=

# The shared mailbox (e.g. ops@cgtechnologies.com) every outbound email is
# sent as, and candidate replies are read from. Uses app-only Graph auth
# with Mail.Send + Mail.ReadWrite, restricted to this one mailbox by an
# Exchange Application Access Policy.
#
# Leaving this blank disables ALL outbound email: every send path fails
# with "SHARED_MAILBOX_EMAIL isn't set". That is deliberate and is the
# right setting for staging — sends also write to the real mailbox's Sent
# Items, so a staging send would be visible to the whole team.
SHARED_MAILBOX_EMAIL=

# --- Web Push -----------------------------------------------------------
# One VAPID pair per deployment. NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY must match
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY exactly: the browser needs the public half to subscribe,
# the server needs both to sign and encrypt. Push is silently skipped when
# either server-side half is missing. Generate a separate pair per
# environment — subscriptions are per-origin anyway.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Contact address a push service may use to reach the app owner about this
# key. Defaults to mailto:ops@cgtechnologies.com if unset.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:ops@cgtechnologies.com

# --- Proposal view tracking ---------------------------------------------
# Salt for the SHA-256 hash of a prospect's IP on the public proposal page
# (the raw IP is never stored). Falls back to CRON_SECRET, then to a
# literal string, so set it explicitly anywhere the hashes matter.
PROPOSAL_VIEW_IP_SALT=

# --- Authentication attempt recording ------------------------------------
# Salt for the SHA-256 hashes in the auth_attempts table (migration 152):
# the submitted email address and the client IP of every sign-in and MFA
# attempt. Neither is ever stored in the clear.
#
# Unlike PROPOSAL_VIEW_IP_SALT above, this has NO fallback. If it is unset
# the app logs an error and records nothing, rather than hashing with a
# constant — a hardcoded fallback salt is no salt, and a table that looks
# protected but isn't is worse than an empty one. Sign-in keeps working
# either way; recording must never be able to break authentication.
#
# Deliberately a DIFFERENT value from PROPOSAL_VIEW_IP_SALT: sharing one
# would let the two tables be joined on ip_hash, linking a prospect reading
# a proposal to a portal sign-in from the same address.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_ATTEMPT_SALT=
